# The repo stores LF and every checkout gets LF, whatever `core.autocrlf` says.
# Without this, a Windows clone rewrites line endings on the way out and the
# test suite goes red in three separate ways: source walks that split on "\n}\n"
# stop matching, generated packaging text stops comparing equal to LICENSE, and
# the signed updater fixtures below no longer hash to what was signed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Built-in hunk-header drivers, so an `@@` line names the enclosing item. Git
# ships these but applies none of them by extension; a file only gets one by
# opting in here. `.slint` has to go without: a custom driver is an `xfuncname`
# in gitconfig, which is not versioned and so reaches no other clone.
*.rs diff=rust
*.py diff=python
*.md diff=markdown

# Signed payloads and their signatures — byte-exact or the verification tests
# are testing nothing. The path rule is for `test-manifest.json`, signed like the
# rest but carrying an extension that is text everywhere else in the tree:
# regenerate it on a host that writes CRLF, sign those bytes, and `text=auto`
# would normalise the payload out from under its own signature on the way into
# the index. Scoped to the payloads — the `regen-*.sh` beside them are scripts
# and want the normalising. `-merge` for the same reason one step later: a
# three-way merge of a signed payload yields a file that still parses and no
# longer verifies, and regenerating plus re-signing is the only resolution, so
# git should refuse rather than guess.
src/services/updater/tests/fixtures/*.json -text -merge
*.bin     binary
*.minisig binary

# The shipped updater pubkey wants that same byte-exactness, but not `binary`'s
# `-diff`: rotating the key is the one change in this tree that most needs a
# human reading the diff, and `binary` renders it as "Binary file not shown".
# The three test pubkeys ride along; a diff of base64 costs nothing.
*.b64     -text -merge

# The vendored winit fork's X11 fixture — a captured xsettings blob with no NUL in
# the first 8000 bytes, so git's heuristic reads it as text and would happily
# rewrite a CR inside a length-prefixed record.
*.dat     binary

# Media, fonts and images. Git's own heuristic already catches most of these;
# spelling them out is what keeps a new fixture from depending on it.
*.flac binary
*.mp3  binary
*.m4a  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.wav  binary
*.aiff binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary

# Linguist, so nothing here changes a checkout. The vendored fork is a large
# share of the tracked Rust and is not ours to be measured on, and top-level
# `winit/` is not on linguist's own vendor list. The lockfile is generated, and
# folding it shut is what keeps it from burying a review.
winit/**   linguist-vendored
Cargo.lock linguist-generated=true
